    "result_text": "I agree with the finding and fixed it. The tests are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe review was right: I had copied the `Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD` table into `PostcodeRules` while an identical one already lived in `OwnerMapper`, so the \"single source of truth\" claim was false \u2014 the two would need hand-syncing.\n\n- **New `util/CityRegions.java`** \u2014 now the one place that holds the city\u2192region table, exposing `regionOf(city)` (returns the region, or `null` for an unknown city). I put it in `util` alongside `AddressNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer`, matching the existing idiom of shared helpers the mapper calls.\n- **`PostcodeRules`** \u2014 dropped its own `CITY_REGION` map; `isValidForCity` now calls `CityRegions.regionOf(city)`. It keeps only the region\u2192range table, which is genuinely postcode-specific. Updated the Javadoc to stop claiming ownership of the region table.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 removed its `CITY_REGION` constant; `toLocality` now delegates to `CityRegions.regionOf(...)`, defaulting to `\"UNKNOWN\"` when null. Dropped the now-unused `java.util.Map` import.\n\nBehaviour is identical \u2014 both `locality` derivation and postcode range validation now read from the same table, so adding or renaming a city updates both at once. The dedicated `CityRegions` helper (rather than having `toLocality` depend on `PostcodeRules`) keeps the semantics clean: locality derivation shouldn't route through a postcode-rules type.",
