        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new code duplicates the city\u2192region table that already exists in the codebase, which breaks the single-source-of-truth property it claims to provide.\n\n**Finding: `CITY_REGION` is duplicated across `PostcodeRules` and `OwnerMapper`.**\n\n- `OwnerMapper` (`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java:31-33`) already defines the fixed city-to-region table and uses it to derive `locality`:\n  ```java\n  /** Fixed city-to-region table used to derive an owner's {@code locality}. */\n  Map<String, String> CITY_REGION = Map.of(\n      \"Sydney\", \"NSW\", \"Melbourne\", \"VIC\", \"Brisbane\", \"QLD\");\n  ```\n- `PostcodeRules` (`src/main/java/.../util/PostcodeRules.java:34-35`) now defines a second, character-for-character identical copy of that same table \u2014 and its own Javadoc admits this: *\"the region is looked up \u2026 in the **same** fixed city-to-region table used to derive `locality`.\"*\n\nSo the same business fact (which city belongs to which region) now lives in two places that must be hand-synced. `PostcodeRules`'s class Javadoc calls itself the *\"single source of truth,\"* but it is not \u2014 adding or renaming a city in one map silently diverges the other (e.g. a new city would get a `locality` region but no postcode validation, or vice versa). A maintainer would not expect two canonical region tables.\n\n**Suggested fix:** extract the city\u2192region mapping to one shared location and have both `toLocality(...)` and `PostcodeRules.isValidForCity(...)` consult it. Given the existing idiom (the normalizers `AddressNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer` already live in `util` and are called by the mapper), the natural home is a single `util` type (e.g. the lookup could live alongside the range table in `PostcodeRules`, or in a small dedicated `CityRegions` helper) that `OwnerMapper.toLocality` then delegates to \u2014 removing the constant from the mapper. The rest of the change (class-level `@PostcodeValidation` for the cross-field rule, the thin `PostcodeValidator` delegating to the rules type, the field/getter/setter on `Owner`, the schema and OpenAPI additions) is idiomatic and well-placed."
